{"type":"message","id":"f6070977","parentId":"2573fe1f","timestamp":"2026-06-16T11:28:17.258Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now the monitor's next update_trailing_stop cycle should detect the BNB SL and sync it to Binance. But this will only happen at the next tick. Let me check: will the monitor pick this up in its next cycle?\n\nLooking at the code flow:\n1. `update_trailing_stop(positions, prices)` runs every cycle\n2. It would see BNB SL=617.81\n3. But the function compares new_sl with pos[\"sl_price\"]: it's updating SL based on trailing logic, not syncing from file\n4. The SL to Binance sync happens inside `sync_stop_loss_to_binance` which is only called when update_trailing_stop actually decides to change the SL\n\nSo the monitor won't automatically sync the SL to Binance just because the file was updated. The monitor's in-memory copy of positions might differ from the file.\n\nLet me think about what will happen:\n1. Monitor loads positions into memory at startup\n2. I modified crypto_positions.json on disk\n3. Monitor already has positions in memory (loaded during startup)\n4. Next cycle: update_trailing_stop checks positions in memory\n5. Check: for BNB, sl_price in memory is 0.0\n6. For BNB SHORT: peak_p=pos.get(\"peak_price\", entry)\n7. peak_price in memory was 0 (from startup recovery failure)\n8. So peak_p = entry = 633 (when peak_price is 0)\n9. gap = tp_pct * tier2.gap_ratio = 0.04 * 0.25 = 0.01\n10. new_sl = 633 * 1.01 = 639.33\n11. pos[\"sl_price\"] is 0.0 (in memory)... wait, new_sl(639.33) < old_sl(0.0)? NO! 639.33 > 0.0\n12. So condition `new_sl < pos[\"sl_price\"]` → 639.33 < 0.0 → FALSE → no update!\n\nThat's bad. The SL stays at 0.0 in memory and never gets synced to Binance!\n\nHmm, but the positions in memory are the ones loaded at startup. Let me check: was the SL already 0.0 at startup? From the earlier check, crypto_positions.json showed BNB SL=0.0 and peak_price=0. So after startup, the monitor's in-memory positions had BNB SL=0.0 and peak_price=0.\n\nNow I've updated the file, but the monitor's in-memory copy is still old. However, there's a periodic API sync (every 20 ticks = 5 minutes). Let me check if that resyncs...\n\nActually, looking at the code, the periodic sync reads from Binance API (not from the local file). The sync section (every 20 ticks) compares Binance positions with local, and calls calc_tp_sl again for any discrepancies.\n\nSo there are two issues:\n1. BNB has no SL on Binance (the Algo order was never created because SL=0.0 in memory)\n2. The in-memory position has SL=0.0 and peak_price=0\n\nThe best fix is to force a restart (kj) so the monitor re-reads crypto_positions.json with the corrected values. Let me tell老公 to restart.\n\nWait, but before suggesting restart, let me check if the monitor could self-heal. Actually, in the update_trailing_stop function, for the SHORT position:\n\n```python\nnew_sl = peak_p * (1 + gap)\nif new_sl < pos[\"sl_price\"]:\n```\n\nIf pos[\"sl_price\"] is 0.0 and new_sl is ~639, then 639 < 0.0 is False, so it won't update. SL will stay at 0.0 forever.\n\nThe only way to fix is a restart that re-reads the file. Let me tell老公.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"数据修好了，但内存里的还是旧的——monitor 不会从文件热加载 SL。再 `kj` 一次让它读到我修好的数据就行。重启后 BNB SL 会回到 617.8，然后三档移动止盈从 3.37% 峰值继续跑 👑"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"deepseek","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","usage":{"input":112,"output":1778,"cacheRead":66816,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":68706,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1781609276290}}
