{"type":"message","id":"16127a09","parentId":"44fa6dda","timestamp":"2026-06-16T11:35:34.945Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The script says logs are at `/root/.openclaw/logs/trading/monitor.log`. But earlier the monitor was launched with `nohup python3 crypto_signal_monitor.py >/dev/null 2>&1 &` so output goes to /dev/null. The trading log might be from a different mechanism.\n\nWait, looking more carefully at line 308: `nohup python3 crypto_signal_monitor.py >/dev/null 2>&1 &`\n\nAll stdout and stderr go to /dev/null! So there's no log file from the monitor's output. That makes debugging harder.\n\nBut looking at line 355, it mentions `/root/.openclaw/logs/trading/monitor.log` from the watchdog. Let me check if there are any recent logs.\n\nActually, the Python monitor might have its own logging setup. Let me check the monitor's logging configuration.\n\nLet me focus on the actual problem instead of log hunting. The BNB SL is 0.0 in crypto_positions.json. I need to understand why.\n\nLet me re-examine the code flow. Maybe the issue is that my manual fix was written to disk, but the kj restart happened and the file was overwritten by the old startup code path BEFORE my fix was applied.\n\nWait, actually, looking at it again: the kj script kills the old monitor and starts a new one. The new monitor reads crypto_positions.json at startup. If my manual fix was written BEFORE the kj, it should have been read. If the kj happened and my fix was written AFTER, the old startup code ran without my fix.\n\nBut from the conversation timeline:\n1. 19:27 - I queried positions, found BNB SL=0\n2. 19:28 - I manually fixed crypto_positions.json \n3. 19:28 - I told老公 to kj\n4. 19:32 - 老公 implied restart was done\n5. 19:33 - I checked and BNB SL was still 0\n\nSo the fix should have been in place before the restart. Unless the monitor was already running and re-saved the file after my fix but before restart.\n\nWait, actually that's possible