{"type":"message","id":"172b0f7c","parentId":"3471cb37","timestamp":"2026-06-16T11:37:51.542Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"NOW I see a potential issue. Let me re-read the try block carefully:\n\n```python\ntry:\n    # ... all position rebuild code ...\n    for pos in positions:\n        submit_initial_algo_orders(pos)\nexcept Exception as e:\n    log.error(f\"❌ 持仓同步失败：{e}\")\n    positions = load_positions()  # 回退到本地文件\n```\n\nIf `submit_initial_algo_orders(pos)` throws an exception for ANY position, the entire try block fails, and it falls back to `load_positions()` from the local file. At that point, the file STILL has the old broken data (before my fix could take effect).\n\nBut wait, the except also has `load_positions()` which would read whatever is currently in the file. If my fix is in the file at that point, it would be read.\n\nActually, the key question is: does `submit_initial_algo_orders` throw? Let me check each step:\n\n```python\ncancel_algo_orders(sym_usdt)  # could throw if network error\nplace_algo_conditional_order(...)  # could throw if network error\n```\n\nIf either of these throws, the entire try block catches it and falls back to the file. But then the file at that point still has SL=0.0 for BNB (since the save happened at the start and wasn't overwritten yet).\n\nBut wait, there are TWO saves in the try block:\n1. One at the end of position rebuild (after loop)\n2. One after peak_pnl_map restore\n\nThe first save happens BEFORE submit_initial_algo_orders. So if submit_initial_algo_orders throws, the file at least has the correct data from the first save.\n\nLet me re-read the code:\n```python\nsave_positions(positions)  # save 1 - positions have correct SL\n# orphan cleanup (has its own try/except)\nfor pos in positions:\n    submit_initial_algo_orders(pos)  # if this throws, goes to except\n```\n\nSo if submit_initial_algo_orders throws, save 1 is already done (with correct SL). The except would do `positions = load_positions()` which would read back the correct data. So this shouldn't cause the SL=0 issue.\n\nUnless... save 1 was called but the data was still wrong at that point. Let me think about what could have gone wrong.\n\nOH WAIT. I just realized something. The `save_positions(positions)` on line 2396 in the ORIGINAL code. But in MY MODIFIED code, there are two save_positions calls:\n- One after position rebuild: `save_positions(positions)` - should have BNB SL=617.81\n- One after peak_pnl_map restore: `save_positions(positions)` - only modifies peak_pnl\n\nBut what if the PEAK_PNL_MAP restore is overwriting the sl_price? NO, it only sets peak_pnl.\n\nOK, I'm going to take a completely different approach. Let me just:\n1. Fix the code to be more resilient\n2. Force-fix BNB SL via direct Binance Algo order creation\n3. Fix the file\n\nFor the code fix, I need to handle the case where old_sl is 0 but old data has valid peak_pnl. In that case, calculate SL from entry using calc_tp_sl default (not 0).\n\nFor the BNB fix, let me directly create the stop loss Algo order on Binance AND update the local file.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"BNB 的旧 SL 是 0，导致启动代码的 `old_sl > 0` 检查直接跳过了恢复。得加固一下：old_sl 为 0 但有 peak 数据时，用 calc_tp_sl 兜底而不是留 0。"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_7QiGWVdJifQPV3rCLcFx6188","name":"edit","arguments":{"file_path":"/root/.openclaw/workspace/scripts/crypto_signal_monitor.py","old_string":"                    if old_direction == direction and old_sl > 0:\n                        # 判断谁的 SL 更紧\n                        if direction == \"SHORT\":\n                            tighter = old_sl < new_sl  # 更低的 SL = 更紧\n                        else:  # LONG\n                            tighter = old_sl > new_sl  # 更高的 SL = 更紧\n                        \n                        if tighter:\n                            sl_price = old_sl\n                            log.info(f\"🔒 {symbol} 保留旧 SL {old_sl:.4f}（比新 SL {new_